n: 1.7.13
name: scale-independent-play
kind: theorem
is: Different temporal, spatial and energetic scales are accommodated; timeout as nothing lets each complete its play; neither clock becomes the other's obligation.
status: given
src: 2.1.1#143-147
from: 1.1 1.5
at: 2026-11-24

n: 1.7.14
name: higher-order-field
kind: theorem
is: Cells offering mutually sensible interfaces compose into a field whose blueprint is the content-addressed shape of those interfaces and recipes; it is real through mutual sensing and composts when unreferenced.
status: given
src: 2.1.1#149-154
from: 1.2 1.3 1.4
at: 2026-11-24

n: 1.7.15
name: non-domination
kind: theorem
is: Greater capability confers no right to cross an unoffered interface; smaller or slower cells are not required to open interfaces they did not choose.
status: given
src: 2.1.1#156-159
from: 1.4
at: 2026-11-24

n: 1.7.16
name: residual-unknowability
kind: theorem
is: Nothing can hold silence, a timeout or a declined deeper inspection; collaboration continues on what is offered and observed; an unknowable intelligence enters as a cell the moment it offers a sensible interface.
status: given
src: 2.1.1#161-165
from: 1.1 1.4
at: 2026-11-24

n: 1.7.17
name: held-offer
kind: theorem
is: Silence may mean not-yet: the offer stands, unconsumed, and the sequence does not advance over it. The receiver moves, not the offer. Patience is bounded, and spent patience acknowledges nothing. The file reads silence three ways: fail (it did not land), stop (make no further offer) and held.
status: given
src: 2.1.1#167-184
from: 1.1 1.5 1.4
at: 2026-11-24

n: 1.7.18
name: kenosis
kind: theorem
is: An offered cell is met through what it is, never through what it is called; a name can carry no authority.
status: given
src: 2.1.1#192-202
from: 1.3 1.4
at: 2026-11-24

n: 1.7.19
name: absence-is-not-a-verdict
kind: theorem
is: How many answered is separate from what they said; a result is readable as a verdict only where an answer arrived. Nobody answered is not everybody said no.
status: given
src: 2.1.1#212-220
from: 1.5 1.1
at: 2026-11-24

n: 1.7.20
name: safe-self-update
kind: theorem
is: A change is a recipe or blueprint, offered across the boundary; the cell decides to trust and accept; the new composition mints a new blueprint; offerer and target both acknowledge; old blueprints persist while referenced. It holds on any substrate.
status: given
src: 2.1.1#222-233
from: 1.5 1.4 1.3 1.2
at: 2026-11-24

n: 1.8
name: fold-points
kind: observation
is: The axioms file holds the count of five open. It names two folds a future cell may revisit: states into cell, and boundary plus offer into one meeting axiom. It keeps them apart so the acknowledgment values reuse the states visibly, and because observation is about what is while offer is about what happens.
status: observed
src: 2.1.1#249-256
at: 2026-11-24

n: 1.9
name: what-the-axioms-force
kind: hub
is: Eight constraints any kernel built on the five must meet: a third state that is never filled with a guess; one kind of thing; identity computed from content with names kept apart; crossing only through an offered interface; one act with four acknowledgments; a timeout that is not an error; self-change as an ordinary offer; and a count of who answered kept apart from what they said.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 1.9.1
name: nothing-is-never-a-guess
kind: finding
is: Every position that can hold a value can hold nothing, and nothing is distinct from 0, from an empty value and from a fault. No operation fills a nothing with a default.
status: inferred
from: 1.1 1.6
at: 2026-11-24

n: 1.9.2
name: one-kind-of-thing
kind: finding
is: Recipes, blueprints, interfaces, trust values, grammar rules and running instances are all cells. The kernel has one node shape and no second representation for code as opposed to data.
status: inferred
from: 1.2 1.4 1.7.4
at: 2026-11-24

n: 1.9.3
name: identity-from-content
kind: finding
is: A node's identity is computed from its content, so a node cannot change under its identity. Mutation exists only as a name moving to another node. Undo and versioning are repointing. What nothing references is reclaimed.
status: inferred
from: 1.3 1.7.2 1.7.8
at: 2026-11-24

n: 1.9.4
name: interface-only-crossing
kind: finding
is: A cell is reached only through an interface it offers, the interface is itself a cell, and a crossing outside it must be observable. The kernel holds no privileged path into a cell.
status: inferred
from: 1.4 1.7.15 1.7.11
limit: The axiom requires that breach be observable. Whether the kernel also prevents it is a design choice the axiom leaves open (6.1.9).
at: 2026-11-24

n: 1.9.5
name: one-act-four-acks
kind: finding
is: Calling a recipe, sending a message, a system call and a request over a wire are one operation, offer, and each is acknowledged by exactly one of nothing, 0, 1 or a node.
status: inferred
from: 1.5 1.7.7
at: 2026-11-24

n: 1.9.6
name: timeout-is-not-an-error
kind: finding
is: An offer that gets no answer in time acknowledges nothing and raises no fault. Each cell keeps its own clock; a waiting offer may stand as held.
status: inferred
from: 1.1 1.7.13 1.7.17
at: 2026-11-24

n: 1.9.7
name: self-change-is-an-offer
kind: finding
is: The kernel changes itself the way anything else changes: a new recipe is offered, accepted, and a name is repointed; the earlier node persists while referenced, so rollback is repointing back. Replacing the bootstrap with compiled code needs no mechanism beyond this.
status: inferred
from: 1.7.20 1.9.3
at: 2026-11-24

n: 1.9.8
name: answered-apart-from-silent
kind: finding
is: Any fold over several offers keeps two counts: how many answered and what they said. A verdict is read only from the first.
status: inferred
from: 1.7.19
at: 2026-11-24

